<p class="isSelectedEnd">The <strong>cost of a living room renovation</strong> can vary a lot depending on what you actually want to change. A simple update like repainting walls, replacing flooring, or upgrading lighting is usually fairly straightforward. But once you start changing the layout, adding bespoke joinery, or altering walls, the project becomes significantly more complex, and more expensive.</p>
<p class="isSelectedEnd">One of the most common mistakes homeowners make is focusing on the price of individual items before understanding what the room needs as a whole. A new sofa won’t fix a poor layout, and expensive flooring won’t make an awkward space feel functional. Comparing wider financial benchmarks like <a href="https://www.oraanj-interiors.co.uk/how-much-does-it-cost-to-renovate-a-house-in-the-uk-in-2026/">how much it costs to renovate a house in the UK in 2026</a> provides helpful planning context.</p>

<h2>How Much Does a Living Room Renovation Cost?</h2>
<p class="isSelectedEnd">There is no fixed price for a living room renovation because every project is different.</p>
<p class="isSelectedEnd">As a general guide:</p>

<ul data-spread="false">
 	<li>A light refresh (painting, flooring, basic lighting updates) may cost a few thousand pounds</li>
 	<li>A mid-range renovation (new electrics, plastering, better lighting design, storage solutions) often falls around £10,000–£20,000+</li>
 	<li>A full renovation with structural changes, bespoke joinery, and high-end finishes can go well beyond that, aligning closely with typical <a href="https://www.oraanj-interiors.co.uk/high-end-house-renovation-cost-london/">high end house renovation costs in London</a></li>
</ul>
<p class="isSelectedEnd">If you are removing walls, reworking the layout, or combining the living room with other spaces, the cost increases further.</p>
<p class="isSelectedEnd">The key point is this: <strong>the scope of work matters more than the size of the room.</strong></p>

